00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:23.478
to just minutes or hours or
at most a very small number

27
00:01:23.478 --> 00:01:24.116
of days.

28
00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:27.973
In the traditional
supervised learning approach,

29
00:01:27.973 --> 00:01:32.290
if you needed to collect say, 10,000
labeled examples anyway, then

30
00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:35.429
the incremental cost of
collecting another 1,000 test

31
00:01:35.429 --> 00:01:39.746
examples isn't that bad. So, in the
traditional supervised learning setting,

32
00:01:39.746 --> 00:01:42.493
it was not
unusual to collect a

33
00:01:42.493 --> 00:01:45.632
training set, collect a development
set or holdout

34
00:01:45.632 --> 00:01:48.772
cross-validation set in the
test set and then

35
00:01:48.772 --> 00:01:51.911
tap those at hand throughout this development process.


36
00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:51.911


37
00:01:52.693 --> 00:01:56.919
But if you're able to specify a prompt
in just minutes and get

38
00:01:56.919 --> 00:02:00.171
something working in hours, then
it would seem like a

39
00:02:00.171 --> 00:02:03.422
huge pain if you had
to pause for a long

40
00:02:03.422 --> 00:02:06.023
time to collect 1,000 test
examples because you

41
00:02:06.023 --> 00:02:08.624
cannot get this working with zero
training examples.

42
00:02:09.546 --> 00:02:12.136
So, when building an
application using an LLM,

43
00:02:12.136 --> 00:02:16.021
this is what it often feels like.
First, you would tune the

44
00:02:16.021 --> 00:02:18.611
prompts on just a
small handful of examples,

45
00:02:18.611 --> 00:02:21.849
maybe one to three to
five examples and try to

46
00:02:21.849 --> 00:02:24.115
get a prompt that works on them.

47
00:02:25.057 --> 00:02:28.069
And then as you have
the system undergo additional testing,

48
00:02:28.069 --> 00:02:31.081
you occasionally run into a
few examples that are tricky.

49
00:02:31.081 --> 00:02:34.394
The prompt doesn't
work on them or the algorithm doesn't work

50
00:02:34.394 --> 00:02:34.997
on them.

51
00:02:35.658 --> 00:02:39.230
And in that case, you can take these
additional one or two

52
00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:41.611
or three or five
examples and add them

53
00:02:41.611 --> 00:02:44.885
to the set that you're testing on to
just add additional

54
00:02:44.885 --> 00:02:45.778
tricky examples opportunistically.


55
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.800
Eventually,
you have enough of these examples you've

56
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.301
added to your slowly growing development
set that it becomes a bit

57
00:02:54.301 --> 00:02:56.551
inconvenient to manually
run every example

58
00:02:56.551 --> 00:02:59.926
through the prompt
every time you change the prompt.

59
00:02:59.926 --> 00:02:59.926


60
00:03:00.427 --> 00:03:03.210
And then you start
to develop metrics to

61
00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.992
measure performance on this
small set of examples,

62
00:03:05.992 --> 00:03:09.123
such as maybe average accuracy.
And one interesting aspect

63
00:03:09.123 --> 00:03:13.297
of this process is,
if you decide at any moment in time,

64
00:03:13.297 --> 00:03:17.471
your system is working well enough,
you can stop right there and

65
00:03:17.471 --> 00:03:19.906
not go on to the next bullet.

66
00:03:20.527 --> 00:03:23.736
And in fact, there are many
deploy applications that

67
00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:28.014
stops at maybe the first or
the second bullet and are running

68
00:03:28.014 --> 00:03:29.084
actually just fine.

69
00:03:30.386 --> 00:03:33.940
Now, if your hand-built
development set that you're evaluating

70
00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:37.888
the model on isn't giving you
sufficient confidence yet in

71
00:03:37.888 --> 00:03:41.047
the performance of your
system, then that's when

72
00:03:41.047 --> 00:03:46.574
you may go to the next
step of collecting a randomly sampled set of

73
00:03:46.574 --> 00:03:48.943
examples to tune the
model to.

74
00:03:49.625 --> 00:03:53.713
And this would continue to be
a development set or a hold-out

75
00:03:53.713 --> 00:03:55.757
cross-validation set, because it'd
be quite

76
00:03:55.757 --> 00:03:58.823
common to continue to tune your
prompt to this.

77
00:03:59.985 --> 00:04:03.338
And only if you need even
higher fidelity estimate

78
00:04:03.338 --> 00:04:08.553
of the performance of your system,
then might you collect and use a hold-out

79
00:04:08.553 --> 00:04:13.395
test sets that you don't even
